'use strict';
// English presentation overlays. IDs, answers, filters, evidence, packet bytes,
// decoded fields, and stored progress always come from the canonical content.
const lessons={};
function L(id,title,intro,mission,hints,explanation,tasks){lessons[id]={title,intro,mission,hints,explanation,tasks:tasks.map(([prompt,why,options])=>({prompt,why,...(options?{options}:{})}))};}
L('ops-view-1','Open your first packet','A capture records traffic observed at a particular location. Each row is one packet. Source is the sender; Destination is the recipient. Selecting a row updates both the details and bytes below.','Select No. 1, read its source IP address, and add that field as evidence.',['Click No. 1 in the packet list.','Open Internet Protocol Version 4 and find Source Address.','ip.src is 10.0.0.10. Select the field, then click Add evidence.'],'The list gives a summary; the details provide supporting fields. No. 1 has ip.src=10.0.0.10, identifying its source IP. An IP address alone does not identify a person or device owner.',[['What is the source IP address of No. 1?','The value is recorded under No. 1 → IPv4 → Source Address.']]);
L('ops-view-2','Go from summary to details','DNS queries information about names. The Info column summarizes a packet, but you can verify it in the details. Query Name is the name being queried.','Open the DNS details of No. 1 and read the query name.',['Open Domain Name System in No. 1.','Find dns.qry.name, which belongs to Queries.','The name is portal.test. Add dns.qry.name as evidence.'],'The client asks its DNS server about portal.test. This packet alone does not show whether name resolution or a subsequent web connection succeeded.',[['Which name was queried?','DNS Query Name identifies the requested name.']]);
L('ops-view-3','Check the direction of a reply','Source and destination may swap between a request and its response. Verify IP addresses and protocol fields instead of relying on row order.','Read the DNS reply and identify the device receiving it.',['Select No. 2, marked DNS response.','Check IPv4 Destination Address.','No. 2 has ip.dst=10.0.0.10.'],'No. 2 is a reply from DNS server 10.0.0.53 to client 10.0.0.10. Comparing the directions of No. 1 and No. 2 reveals the exchange.',[['What is the destination IP of the DNS response?','The response Destination Address identifies the recipient.']]);
L('ops-filter-1','Show only DNS','A display filter shows only matching packets from a loaded capture. It does not delete the original data. Entering dns matches packets containing DNS.','Enter dns in the display filter and apply it.',['Use the Display filter input above the list.','Enter dns and click Apply.','The four matching packets are No. 1, 3, 4, and 5.'],'Four packets contain DNS. Clearing the filter brings NTP and ICMP back into view. A display filter differs from a capture filter used during collection.',[['Show only the four packets containing DNS.','DNS requests and responses remain; NTP and ICMP are excluded.']]);
L('ops-filter-2','Combine a source and a protocol','ip.src is the source IP. && means both conditions must match. ip.addr matches either source or destination; use ip.src or ip.dst when direction matters.','Show only DNS packets sent by 10.0.0.11.',['Combine dns and ip.src.','Write the address condition as ip.src == 10.0.0.11.','dns && ip.src == 10.0.0.11 leaves only No. 4.'],'No. 4 is a query from 10.0.0.11. No. 5 is a reply to that client, but its source is the DNS server, so it does not match.',[['Show only DNS sent by 10.0.0.11.','Only No. 4 contains DNS and has the specified source.']]);
L('ops-filter-3','Keep both directions','ip.addr matches the source or destination. Different filter expressions can have the same meaning. No. is the original frame number and stays unchanged when filtering.','Show only the DNS exchange involving 10.0.0.10.',['Filter by both DNS and the target client.','Use ip.addr to include both directions.','dns && ip.addr == 10.0.0.10 shows No. 1 and 3.'],'No. 1 and 3 form the target DNS exchange. UDP port 53 can also select them in this capture, but a port number alone does not prove the application protocol in real traffic.',[['Show only the DNS exchange involving 10.0.0.10.','Both the query and its response are visible.']]);
L('base-ethernet-1','Find the source MAC address','Ethernet delivers frames on the same link. A MAC address looks like 02:10:00:00:00:20. Even when the list shows an IP source, Ethernet has its own source address.','Open packet 1 and select Ethernet II → Source as evidence.',['Select No. 1 and open Ethernet II.','Source means sender. Look under Ethernet II, not Internet Protocol.','eth.src is 02:10:00:00:00:20. Add that field as evidence.'],'Packet 1 has Ethernet source 02:10:00:00:00:20. Packet 2 reverses source and destination. Ethernet, IP, and UDP each provide addressing information at their own layer. These fictional MACs begin with 02 and are locally administered. With MAC name resolution enabled, Wireshark may show vendor names for registered prefixes; the underlying address remains available in the details.',[['What is the source MAC address of packet 1?','eth.src is the Ethernet source, separate from the IP address and UDP port.']]);
L('base-ethernet-2','Find a broadcast','Ethernet destination ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff is a broadcast to the local broadcast domain. Other frames can have individual destinations.','Compare Ethernet destinations and select the broadcast frames.',['Read Ethernet II → Destination.','Check eth.dst, not just the ARP protocol label. The ARP reply here is unicast.','Only packet 2 is addressed to ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff.'],'Packet 2 is the broadcast. The sender does not yet know the target MAC, so this ARP request uses Ethernet broadcast. This does not mean broadcasting across the entire Internet.',[['Select all Ethernet broadcast packets.','Use the Ethernet destination. The ARP reply in packet 3 targets one device.']]);
L('base-ethernet-3','IP destination and MAC destination','For a destination on another network, a client normally first hands the frame to its gateway. This capture is on the client LAN. Read the Ethernet destination and the IP peer separately.','Report both destination addresses in packet 3.',['Open Ethernet II and Internet Protocol Version 4 in packet 3.','eth.dst is the next recipient on this link; ip.dst is the IP destination. The ARP reply helps map the addresses.','eth.dst is 02:10:00:00:03:01; ip.dst is 192.0.2.80.'],'The Ethernet destination is gateway 02:10:00:00:03:01; the IPv4 destination is 192.0.2.80. Routers replace link headers, so visible MACs depend on capture location. NAT may also change IP destinations. Always consider where a capture was taken.',[['What is the destination MAC address of packet 3?','On this LAN the frame goes to the gateway MAC; the client is not using ARP for the remote web server.'],['What is the destination IPv4 address in the same packet 3?','The IP destination is the intended web server. MAC and IP describe destinations at different layers.']]);
L('base-arp-1','Read the target of an ARP request','ARP maps IPv4 addresses to MAC addresses on the same link. Sender identifies the requester; Target identifies the address being sought. ARP carries these fields itself, outside an IPv4 header.','Open ARP in packet 1 and use Target IP address as evidence.',['Open Address Resolution Protocol in packet 1.','Sender IP address is the requester. Read Target IP address for the requested mapping.','arp.dst.proto_ipv4 is 10.20.1.1.'],'Packet 1 asks for the MAC of 10.20.1.1. ARP Opcode 1 is Request; 2 is Reply. The target MAC is unknown in this request and is represented as 00:00:00:00:00:00.',[['For which IPv4 address does packet 1 request a MAC address?','ARP Target IP identifies the query target. Ethernet broadcast destination serves a different purpose.']]);
L('base-arp-2','Verify the returned MAC','An ARP reply gives the responding device\'s mapping in Sender IP and Sender MAC. When exchanges overlap, the next reply is not necessarily yours. Check Opcode and Sender IP first.','Find the reply for 10.20.2.60 and add both its IP and MAC as evidence.',['Find a packet with ARP Opcode 2 (reply).','Use the reply whose Sender IP address is 10.20.2.60. Target refers to the requester.','Select arp.src.proto_ipv4 and arp.src.hw_mac in packet 3. The MAC is 02:20:00:00:02:60.'],'Packet 3 claims that 10.20.2.60 has MAC 02:20:00:00:02:60. This is the mapping advertised by the captured ARP reply; ARP alone does not authenticate the device.',[['Which MAC was returned for 10.20.2.60? Select both IP and MAC as evidence.','Pairing Sender IP and Sender MAC shows which address the reply describes. Avoid mixing in another exchange.']]);
L('base-arp-3','Follow one ARP exchange','ARP has no DNS-style transaction ID. Match requests and replies using requester, target, and MAC direction. Two queries are interleaved here.','Select the request for 10.20.3.1, then its reply.',['Find a request with Target IP 10.20.3.1.','Its reply has Sender IP 10.20.3.1 and Target IP 10.20.3.15.','Select packet 2, then packet 4.'],'Packet 2 asks about 10.20.3.1; packet 4 replies with 02:20:00:00:03:01. Packet 5 then goes to that MAC. Follow address relationships, not just adjacent rows.',[['Select the ARP request for 10.20.3.1, followed by its reply.','Packets 1 and 3 concern 10.20.3.99. The target/sender addresses match packets 2 and 4.']]);
L('base-ip-1','Read the IPv4 peers','IPv4 addresses use four numbers, such as 10.30.1.21. Source and Destination identify the IP sender and recipient. Read them separately from Ethernet MACs and TCP ports.','Read the source address in the IPv4 details of packet 2.',['Select No. 2 and open Internet Protocol Version 4.','Source Address is the sender; Destination Address is the recipient.','ip.src is 10.30.1.21, not the MAC in eth.src.'],'The source is 10.30.1.21 and the destination is 203.0.113.40. IPv4 Protocol identifies the next protocol, here TCP (6). TTL decreases during forwarding; observing 64 alone does not establish the OS or the number of routers already crossed.',[['Which IPv4 address sent packet 2?','IPv4 Source Address identifies the sender. The reply in packet 3 reverses the direction.']]);
L('base-ip-2','IPv6 and Hop Limit','IPv6 uses colon-separated notation; :: compresses consecutive zeros. Like IPv4 TTL, Hop Limit decreases on forwarding. It is not a duration or response time.','Read the IPv6 destination and Hop Limit in packet 2.',['Open Internet Protocol Version 6 in packet 2.','Find Destination Address and Hop Limit. Do not confuse them with IPv4 packet 1.','The destination is 2001:db8:30::80; Hop Limit is 32. Use ipv6.dst and ipv6.hlim.'],'The destination is 2001:db8:30::80 and Hop Limit is 32. Next Header identifies what follows. With no extension headers in this example, UDP (17) follows directly.',[['What is the destination IPv6 address of packet 2?','ipv6.dst identifies the destination. The process is the same despite different address notation.'],['What Hop Limit is observed in packet 2?','This is the remaining forwarding limit, not a count of 32 routers already crossed.']]);
L('base-ip-3','Recognize UDP over IPv6','IP version and the encapsulated transport or control protocol are separate properties. Both IPv4 and IPv6 can carry UDP. Inspect the layers instead of relying only on Protocol in the list.','Select all packets with UDP following IPv6.',['Find both IPv6 and User Datagram Protocol.','Packets 1 and 4 carry UDP over IPv4; 3 and 6 carry ICMPv6 over IPv6.','Packets 2 and 5 match. Their ipv6.nxt value is 17.'],'Packets 2 and 5 are IPv6 + UDP; 1 and 4 are IPv4 + UDP; 3 and 6 are IPv6 + ICMPv6. IPv4 Protocol and IPv6 Next Header identify the following header. IPv6 extension headers can appear before UDP.',[['Select all UDP packets carried over IPv6.','Check both the IPv6 network layer and UDP transport layer. A UDP label alone does not identify the IP version.']]);
L('base-icmp-1','Match an Echo request and reply','IPv4 ping usually uses ICMP Echo Request (Type 8) and Echo Reply (Type 0). Match reversed addresses, Identifier, and Sequence Number to distinguish repeated pings.','Select the request and reply for Sequence Number 7, in order.',['Open Internet Control Message Protocol and read Type and Sequence Number.','Find Type 8 and Type 0 with Identifier 401 and Sequence Number 7.','Packet 1 is the request; packet 3 is the reply. Packets 4 and 5 use Sequence Number 8.'],'Packets 1 and 3 form an Echo exchange. This establishes an observed ICMP round trip, not the availability of web or other services. A missing reply could reflect filtering, loss, or capture location rather than a powered-off host. This payload imitates a 32-byte alphabet pattern often used by Windows ping, but payloads can be changed or imitated, so they do not prove the OS.',[['Select Echo Request then Echo Reply for Sequence Number 7.','Identifier, sequence, and address direction match. UDP packet 2 is unrelated.']]);
L('base-icmp-2','Read Destination Unreachable','ICMP also reports communication errors. Type and Code together identify the reason more precisely. Error messages quote part of the original packet.','Use both the outer ICMP Type and Code of packet 2 as evidence.',['Open Internet Control Message Protocol in packet 2.','Type 3 means Destination Unreachable. Also check Code 3.','Type 3 / Code 3 means Port Unreachable. Add icmp.type and icmp.code.'],'Type 3 / Code 3 means Port Unreachable. Here 10.40.2.50 reports a problem with UDP destination port 7099. This does not establish that the entire host is down. The additional IPv4 and UDP headers inside the error quote the original packet.',[['What does packet 2 report?','Type 3 alone is insufficient. Code 3 specifies Port Unreachable.',{'echo-success':'A normal Echo Reply to an Echo Request','port-unreachable':'Destination port unreachable (Port Unreachable)','ttl-expired':'TTL expired in transit (Time Exceeded)','tcp-established':'A TCP connection was established'}]]);
L('base-icmp-3','Recognize TTL expiry','IPv4 TTL decreases at each router. When it expires in transit, a router may discard the packet and return ICMP Time Exceeded. traceroute uses such messages as clues to a route.','Check the transmitted TTL and the returned ICMP reason.',['Read IPv4 TTL in packet 1 and ICMP Type and Code in packet 2.','Type 11 is Time Exceeded; Code 0 means TTL expired in transit.','Packet 1 has ip.ttl=1. Packet 2 has icmp.type=11 and icmp.code=0.'],'The first router returns Type 11 / Code 0 for a packet sent with TTL 1. The packet exhausted its forwarding limit; the capture does not establish whether final destination 203.0.113.90 is operational.',[['What TTL was packet 1 sent with?','Read packet 1 itself, not the header quoted inside the error.'],['What reason does the ICMP message in packet 2 give?','Type 11 / Code 0 is expiry in transit, not a statement about the destination application or host.',{'transit-expired':'TTL expired in transit','echo-reply':'A normal reply to an Echo Request','host-off':'IP fragment reassembly time exceeded','port-unreachable':'The destination UDP port was unreachable'}]]);
L('base-icmp6-1','Find an ICMPv6 Echo Reply','IPv6 uses ICMPv6. Echo Request is Type 128 and Echo Reply is Type 129, unlike IPv4 ICMP. Matching addresses, Identifier, and Sequence Number still works.','Find and select the IPv6 Echo Reply.',['Check Type in the ICMPv6 details.','Type 128 is the request and 129 is the reply. Packet 2 is unrelated UDP.','Packet 3 is an Echo Reply with Type 129.'],'Packet 3 replies to packet 1: IPv6 addresses reverse and Identifier 501 / Sequence Number 3 match. ICMPv6 also supports essential IPv6 operations such as neighbor discovery.',[['Select the ICMPv6 Echo Reply in this capture.','ICMPv6 Echo Reply is Type 129; IPv4 ICMP uses Type 0. Read protocol and Type together.']]);
L('base-icmp6-2','Discover neighbors with NS and NA','IPv6 uses ICMPv6 Neighbor Discovery (NDP), not ARP. Neighbor Solicitation (NS, Type 135) can request a mapping; Neighbor Advertisement (NA, Type 136) can reply. The multicast destination of an NS differs from its Target Address.','Follow the NS for fe80::1 and its matching NA.',['Find an NS with Target Address fe80::1.','Packet 2 has IPv6 destination ff02::1:ff00:1 but NS Target Address fe80::1.','Select NS packet 2, then NA packet 4 with the same target.'],'Packets 2 and 4 form the exchange for fe80::1. The NS goes to solicited-node multicast; the NA returns by unicast. No. 4 sets Router, Solicited, and Override flags, advertising that the sender is a router. NDP supports link-layer address resolution and reachability checks.',[['What Target Address does the NS in packet 2 seek?','The NS target is the sought address. IPv6 destination ff02::1:ff00:1 delivers the multicast query.'],['Select the NS for fe80::1 followed by its NA.','Match Types 135 and 136, Target Address, and direction. Packets 1 and 3 concern fe80::99.']]);
L('base-icmp6-3','Read router information in an RA','Router Advertisement (RA, Type 134) announces router and network information. Router Lifetime is how long the sender can be used as a default router, in seconds. It differs from IPv6 Hop Limit.','Find the RA and read its Router Lifetime.',['Look for ICMPv6 Type 134.','Router lifetime is inside ICMPv6 in packet 2, not IPv6 Hop Limit 255.','icmpv6.nd.ra.router_lifetime is 1800 seconds.'],'Packet 2 advertises a Router Lifetime of 1800 seconds to all-nodes address ff02::1. In No. 5, fe80::1 also replies with an NA carrying the Router flag. RAs can advertise prefixes for SLAAC. DHCPv6 also distributes configuration, but RA provides IPv6 default-router discovery. One RA does not prove which settings each host applied.',[['Select the Router Advertisement packet.','Type 134 identifies RA; Echo and NS also use ICMPv6.'],['What Router Lifetime does this RA advertise, in seconds?','Router Lifetime is the default-router validity period, distinct from address leases, prefix lifetimes, and Hop Limit.']]);
L('base-udp-1','Read direction using UDP ports','UDP carries datagrams. IP identifies the host; ports help distinguish communication endpoints within that host. UDP itself has no TCP-style three-way connection setup.','Compare ports in request packet 1 and response packet 3.',['Open User Datagram Protocol and read Source Port and Destination Port.','Packet 1 goes to port 7070. The response uses 7070 as its source.','udp.dstport is 7070 in packet 1 and 56018 in packet 3.'],'The request is 56018 → 7070; the response is 7070 → 56018. UDP has source port, destination port, length, and checksum fields. Ports alone do not prove an application; also inspect payload and flow.',[['What is the destination UDP port of packet 1?','udp.dstport is the destination. Source Port 56018 belongs to the requester.'],['What is the destination UDP port of response packet 3?','The reply targets requester port 56018. Follow both addresses and ports to distinguish conversations.']]);
L('base-udp-2','Separate header and data lengths','The UDP header is 8 bytes. UDP Length includes this header and UDP data, but excludes Ethernet and IP headers. The list Length describes the whole frame.','Read UDP Length in packet 1 and subtract the 8-byte header.',['Read User Datagram Protocol → Length in packet 1.','UDP Length is 12, including the 8-byte header. Calculate 12 − 8.','The data is 4 bytes. Use udp.length in packet 1 as evidence.'],'12 − 8 = 4 bytes of UDP data, the ASCII string PING. Ethernet 14 + IPv4 20 + UDP 12 = 46 bytes; 14 bytes of padding bring the captured frame to 60. Including the 4-byte FCS, normally omitted from PCAP, Ethernet has a 64-byte minimum on the wire. Padding is not UDP data. A sender-side capture can record a shorter frame before the NIC adds padding.',[['How many UDP data bytes are in packet 1? Use UDP Length as evidence.','Subtract the 8-byte UDP header from udp.length=12. Subtracting from frame length would also include IP and Ethernet.']]);
L('base-udp-3','Who implements replies and retries?','UDP itself guarantees neither delivery nor ordering nor retransmission. Applications can implement their own requests, timeouts, and retries. This example application repeats a request after one second.','Follow the target exchange and count the observed requests and responses.',['Find UDP from 10.60.3.35:56035 to 10.60.3.75:62075.','Packets 1 and 3 are requests; packet 4 is the reverse-direction response. ICMP is unrelated.','Select requests 1 and 3 and the observation “two requests, one response.” Use frame.number from 1, 3, and 4 as evidence.'],'Requests appear in packets 1 and 3; the response is packet 4. We see a response after the second request, but cannot locate any loss from this short capture. DNS, DHCP, and NTP also define application-level request/response behavior over UDP.',[['Select all UDP packets from 10.60.3.35:56035 to 10.60.3.75:62075.','Match IP and port direction. Packet 4 belongs to the exchange but travels in the opposite direction.'],['What does this capture show? Use the three target Frame Number fields as evidence.','Separate observed counts from causes. UDP has no automatic retry mechanism; the example application retries.',{'server-down':'Two request-side UDP packets and one corresponding ICMP error','tcp-before':'Two request-side UDP packets and two response-side UDP packets','observed-count':'Two request-side UDP packets and one response-side UDP packet','udp-auto-retry':'One request-side UDP packet and two response-side UDP packets'}]]);
L('app-tcp-01','Follow connection setup from SYN','TCP normally starts with SYN → SYN/ACK → ACK before transferring data. Follow the same IP/port pair even when DNS rows are interleaved.','Identify the initiator and the three handshake packets.',['Filter with tcp to show only TCP.','Find a packet with SYN=1 and ACK=0 in Flags.','The order is No. 2 → 4 → 5. Read IPv4 Source Address in No. 2 for the initiator.'],'10.0.0.10 sends SYN in No. 2, 10.0.0.80 returns SYN/ACK in No. 4, and the client sends ACK in No. 5. This establishes TCP, but does not prove successful web retrieval.',[['Which IPv4 address initiated the TCP connection?','The sender of the first SYN initiated this connection.'],['Select the three handshake packets in order.','The order is SYN, SYN/ACK, ACK. The two DNS packets are not part of this connection.']]);
L('app-tcp-02','ACK is the next expected byte','TCP Seq and Ack identify byte positions. Relative display starts the SYN at 0 and the next byte at 1. Sequence Number (raw) holds the actual 32-bit value. ACK is the next byte expected.','Match five transmitted bytes with their acknowledgment.',['Find TCP from 10.0.0.10 to 10.0.0.80 with Len greater than 0.','No. 4 contains five bytes, HELLO, starting at relative Seq 1.','Read Acknowledgment Number in No. 5. After bytes 1 through 5, the next expected byte is 6.'],'No. 4 sends five bytes at relative Seq 1; No. 5 acknowledges them with Ack 6. Wireshark calculates relative numbers from each direction\'s initial value without changing packet bytes. The example uses large 32-bit raw numbers. TCP ACK proves receipt at TCP, not that the application processed or saved the content.',[['How many TCP payload bytes does the client send in No. 4?','TCP Segment Len is 5, excluding Ethernet, IP, and TCP headers.'],['What relative ACK in No. 5 acknowledges those five bytes?','Seq 1 plus five bytes makes the next expected byte 6.'],['Select all packets belonging to this TCP connection.','Follow the IP/port pair and TCP Stream. No. 6 is unrelated UDP.']]);
L('app-tcp-03','Distinguish FIN from RST','FIN ends sending in one direction; RST resets a connection. Describe the observed termination separately from its underlying cause.','Find the FIN/ACK closure and the RST in two connections.',['Separate connections by source ports 51030 and 51031.','Try tcp.flags.fin == 1 and tcp.flags.reset == 1.','No. 4 → 5 → 6 → 7 close both directions. No. 12 in the later connection sets RST.'],'No. 5 acknowledges the FIN in No. 4; No. 6 sends the reverse FIN, acknowledged by No. 7. The second connection has an RST in No. 12. RST alone does not establish an attack, server failure, or user action.',[['Select the four packets closing the first connection, in order.','FIN consumes one sequence number. Follow each direction\'s FIN and its ACK.'],['Select the packet with RST set.','Reset is set in the TCP Flags of No. 12.'],['What can you say from No. 12 alone?','No. 12 is a server-to-client RST/ACK. Its flags do not explain why the reset happened.',{'reset-seen':'A server-to-client RST/ACK was observed','attack':'A server-to-client FIN/ACK was observed','power':'A client-to-server RST/ACK was observed'}]]);
L('app-dns-01','Read a name and its IPv4 answer','DNS Query is the question; Answer is the reply data. An A record returns IPv4. Distinguish an answer address from source and destination in the packet\'s IP header.','Read the queried name and the returned IPv4 address.',['Filter with dns to remove NTP and show two DNS packets.','Open Domain Name System → Queries → Name in No. 2.','No. 3 is the reply. Read Address in Answers. IP-header address 10.0.0.53 belongs to the DNS server.'],'The DNS server answers the A query for portal.test with 192.0.2.80. That web-server address differs from DNS server 10.0.0.53. OPT in Additional records carries EDNS(0) extension information, such as supported UDP payload size. It can occur in queries and is not an Answer for the name. A DNS reply does not establish successful web access.',[['Which name was queried?','Read the name in DNS Queries.'],['Which IPv4 address is in the DNS answer?','The Answer A record contains the returned IPv4 address.']]);
L('app-dns-02','AAAA and NXDOMAIN','AAAA records return IPv6 addresses, even when the DNS exchange itself travels over IPv4. NXDOMAIN is a reply code indicating the queried name does not exist.','Distinguish an IPv6 answer from a name-not-found response.',['No. 1 and 2 concern v6.portal.test; No. 3 and 4 concern missing.test.','Read AAAA in No. 2 Answers, and Reply code in No. 4 Flags.','The IPv6 answer is 2001:db8:20::80. No. 4 has rcode=3, NXDOMAIN.'],'v6.portal.test returns AAAA 2001:db8:20::80; missing.test returns NXDOMAIN. NXDOMAIN differs from receiving no response and from an existing name having no record of a requested type.',[['Which IPv6 address was returned for v6.portal.test?','Read the AAAA Address. These DNS packets themselves use IPv4 headers.'],['What does the response code for missing.test mean?','No. 4 is a DNS response with rcode=3, not an HTTP status.',{'no-packet':'No DNS response packet arrived','web-error':'The web server returned 404','name-missing':'The queried name does not exist'}]]);
L('app-dns-03','DNS also travels over TCP','DNS does not use only UDP. This capture contains DNS over both UDP and TCP. Open the protocol layers to verify the transport.','Show only DNS messages over TCP, then read their answer.',['Start with dns; four query/response rows remain.','Combine conditions with dns && tcp.','No. 6 and 8 remain. The Answer in No. 8 contains 192.0.2.44.'],'dns && tcp selects No. 6 and 8. Handshake packets 3–5 and ACKs 7 and 9 are TCP but contain no DNS messages. DNS uses TCP for several reasons; this capture alone does not show a fallback from UDP.',[['Apply a display filter keeping only DNS messages over TCP.','dns && tcp is one solution. Any valid filter selecting the same packet set is accepted.'],['Which IPv4 address was returned in the DNS-over-TCP response?','Read the A record in the DNS response carried over TCP.'],['What is the destination port of the DNS-over-TCP query?','Read TCP Destination Port in No. 6, not a UDP field.']]);
L('app-dhcp-01','Receive configuration with DORA','Typical initial DHCPv4 acquisition is Discover → Offer → Request → ACK. Offer proposes configuration; ACK confirms allocation. Notice 0.0.0.0 from a client that has no IPv4 address yet.','Select the four messages in order and read the acknowledged address.',['Read DHCP → Option (53): DHCP Message Type in each packet.','Discover=1, Offer=2, Request=3, ACK=5; ACK is not 4.','Select No. 1 → 2 → 3 → 4. No. 4 has Your (client) IP address 10.20.0.42.'],'The server offers 10.20.0.42, the client requests it, and the server acknowledges it. Your IP address in ACK is the confirmed allocation. ACK alone does not prove the client OS applied the configuration.',[['Select Discover, Offer, Request, and ACK in order.','Follow DHCP Message Type values 1, 2, 3, and 5.'],['Which IPv4 address did the server acknowledge?','Read Your (client) IP address in the ACK.']]);
L('app-dhcp-02','Read configuration beyond the IP','DHCP can distribute router and DNS settings as well as an IP address. Two clients are interleaved here; use Your IP address to find the correct ACK.','Read the router and DNS settings acknowledged for 10.20.0.42.',['Find the ACK whose Your IP address is 10.20.0.42. There are two ACKs.','No. 7 is the target ACK. Open Router and Domain Name Server options.','Router is 10.20.0.254; DNS is 10.20.0.53. No. 8 belongs to a different client.'],'No. 7 specifies Router 10.20.0.254 and DNS 10.20.0.53. DHCP, router, and DNS roles need not use the same device; DHCP server here is 10.20.0.1. Discover also includes Client identifier, Host Name, Vendor class, and Maximum message size. ACK contains Lease, Renewal, and Rebinding times. Use option numbers and names to find the required settings.',[['Which router IPv4 address was given to 10.20.0.42?','Read the target ACK\'s Router option, separate from the DHCP server IP.'],['Which DNS server IPv4 address is in the same ACK?','Domain Name Server specifies the DNS server to use for name resolution.']]);
L('app-dhcp-03','Follow the choice between two offers','Multiple servers may answer a Discover with offers. Do not treat an Offer as a final assignment: follow Server Identifier in Request and the final ACK.','Identify the selected DHCP server and acknowledged IPv4 address.',['No. 2 and 3 offer different settings for the same Discover.','Read DHCP Server Identifier in Request No. 4.','Request selects 10.30.0.1; ACK No. 5 confirms Your IP address 10.30.0.20.'],'Servers 10.30.0.1 and 10.30.0.2 both offer settings. Request selects 10.30.0.1; ACK No. 5 confirms 10.30.0.20. Multiple offers alone do not establish the presence of a rogue DHCP server.',[['Which DHCP server was selected in Request?','Request\'s Server Identifier gives the selected server.'],['Which IPv4 address was acknowledged?','Use ACK for confirmation, not Offer.'],['Select the ACK confirming allocation.','No. 5 has DHCP Message Type ACK. No. 2 and 3 are offers.']]);
L('app-ntp-01','Match a time request and response','NTP synchronizes time over a network. Match client Mode 3 with server Mode 4, checking UDP ports as well.','Identify the requested time server and its response.',['ntp leaves two rows. You can also compare DNS answers with the NTP destination.','NTP Flags → Mode is Client (3) in No. 3 and Server (4) in No. 4.','No. 3 has IPv4 destination 10.0.0.123. Select No. 4 as the reply.'],'The client sends Mode 3 to 10.0.0.123 and receives Mode 4. UDP has no TCP handshake; an application request/response exchange is distinct from a TCP connection.',[['What is the destination IPv4 address of the NTP request?','The destination of No. 3 is the queried time server.'],['Select the NTP server response.','Check Mode 4 and the reverse IP/port direction.'],['What Mode number is in the selected NTP response?','Server responses in this ordinary client/server exchange use Mode 4.']]);
L('app-ntp-02','Stratum does not guarantee accuracy','Stratum describes distance in the clock hierarchy. Synchronized servers typically advertise 1, 2, 3, and so on, but stratum alone does not measure error or trustworthiness.','Read both servers\' strata and choose a supported observation.',['Read NTP → Peer Clock Stratum in responses No. 2 and 4.','10.0.0.123 advertises 2; 10.0.0.124 advertises 3.','Compare ntp.stratum, not Version or Mode. It is the advertised clock hierarchy.'],'The responses advertise strata 2 and 3. These packets alone do not measure actual clock error. Evaluating synchronization also needs offsets, delay, dispersion, and multiple observations.',[['What stratum does 10.0.0.123 advertise?','Read Peer Clock Stratum in the target server\'s response.'],['What can you say about these two advertised strata?','Strata 2 and 3 differ from Mode 4. Hierarchy alone does not establish accuracy or security.',{'safe':'Both servers advertise clock hierarchy 4','hierarchy':'10.0.0.123 advertises 2; 10.0.0.124 advertises 3','exact':'10.0.0.123 advertises 3; 10.0.0.124 advertises 2'}]]);
L('app-ntp-03','Describe a missing response carefully','A capture records part of the traffic at a particular place and time. When a reply is absent, separate your observations from assumptions about its cause.','Find 10.0.0.10\'s NTP requests and choose an accurate report.',['Use ntp && ip.addr == 10.0.0.10.','Remaining No. 1 and 4 both have Mode 3. No. 6 is another client/server exchange.','Report that this capture has no matching response, rather than declaring the server down.'],'Two requests from 10.0.0.10 to 10.0.0.123 were observed, without matching replies in this recording. Filters, capture location, duration, route, and loss may affect visibility. Server failure cannot be established from this alone.',[['Select only NTP requests sent by 10.0.0.10.','No. 1 and 4 are Mode 3 from the target client; No. 5 and 6 concern another client.'],['After reviewing the whole capture, which report is supported?','Both selected packets are requests. No. 6 replies to 10.0.0.20. More observations are needed to explain the missing target response.',{'not-observed':'The target sent two requests; no corresponding response appears in this recording','down':'The target sent one request and received a server response, not a retry, five seconds later','success':'The target sent two requests, and No. 6 is their server response'}]]);
L('app-http-01','Match GET with 200 OK','Plaintext HTTP exposes Method, Host, URI, and response Status Code. Even with many headers such as Accept and Accept-Language, start with the request line and Host. Check the application result as well as TCP setup.','Read the requested path and its HTTP status code.',['http leaves the request and response. No. 4 is GET; No. 6 is HTTP/1.1 200 OK.','Request URI gives the path; Host gives the destination name.','No. 4 requests /index.html; No. 6 has status 200. These differ from TCP ACK numbers.'],'The client requests portal.test/index.html with GET; the server returns 200 OK and a body. 200 indicates success for this HTTP request, not completion of every image download or the rendered page.',[['Which path was requested by GET?','Read Request URI and combine it with Host to describe the destination.'],['What HTTP status code answers this request?','The HTTP Status Code is 200.']]);
L('app-http-02','Separate POST from the 404','Requests to the same web server can have different methods and outcomes. Plaintext HTTP can also expose a POST body. Follow TCP connections and request/response pairs, not ports alone.','Identify the result for /old.txt and the method for /api/notes.',['http leaves GET, 404, POST, and 201.','/old.txt uses client port 51102; /api/notes uses 51103.','No. 6 returns 404 for /old.txt. No. 11 uses POST for /api/notes; No. 13 returns 201.'],'GET /old.txt receives 404; POST /api/notes receives 201. Even with 404, TCP and an HTTP response succeeded. 404 indicates a missing requested resource, not by itself an attack or a disconnected network. The POST body is message=hello.',[['Which method requests /api/notes?','Read Request Method in the request with the target URI.'],['What status code answers the request for /old.txt?','Match GET with its response on the same TCP connection; do not use the other connection\'s 201.'],['What does No. 6 show?','No. 6 answers GET /old.txt with 404. A missing resource differs from failed communication.',{'tcp-failed':'An HTTP 200 response to GET /old.txt','attack':'An HTTP 404 response to POST /api/notes','http-response':'An HTTP 404 response to GET /old.txt'}]]);
L('app-http-03','Reassemble a split response','One HTTP message can span multiple TCP segments. Use reassembled HTTP details and Follow stream instead of judging a single row in isolation.','Find both response-data packets and read the reassembled HTTP result.',['Find server 10.0.0.80 → client TCP packets with TCP Segment Len greater than 0.','No. 6 and 8 carry response bytes; No. 7 is unrelated UDP.','No. 8 completes reassembly, revealing status 200. Follow stream also shows body packet-reading.'],'No. 6 and 8 carry the split response. Once No. 8 arrives, a complete HTTP message yields 200 OK and its body. Follow sequence numbers within one connection even when unrelated traffic is interleaved.',[['Select the two server packets carrying HTTP response data.','No. 6 and 8 have server-side TCP payload. Exclude pure ACKs and unrelated UDP.'],['What is the reassembled HTTP response status?','Use HTTP details in No. 8, where reassembly completes.']]);
L('app-tls-01','Read a name in ClientHello','TLS ClientHello may carry a server name in SNI. This example contains a short, unencrypted TLS 1.2 ClientHello. Real browsers may propose TLS 1.3 with many cipher suites and extensions, making messages several times larger. Find server_name and distinguish it from the IP destination.','Read SNI and the packet destination IP in this ClientHello.',['Open TLS → Handshake Protocol: Client Hello in No. 4.','Server Name inside Extension: server_name is SNI.','SNI is secure.test. IPv4 Destination Address in the same packet is 198.51.100.80.'],'This ClientHello exposes SNI secure.test, a requested server name rather than a URL path or HTTP body. SNI may be absent, and mechanisms can encrypt part of ClientHello; a plaintext name is not always observable.',[['Which name is in this ClientHello\'s SNI?','Use the ClientHello server_name extension.'],['What is the destination IPv4 address of this ClientHello?','Read the IPv4 destination, a different layer from SNI.']]);
L('app-tls-02','Know the limits of encrypted content','This recording begins mid-conversation and includes opaque teaching data shaped as TLS Application Data. No decryption information is available. Separate readable headers from information requiring plaintext content.','Identify the sender and decide whether the HTTP URL path is readable.',['Select No. 2. IP and TCP headers are visible, but no HTTP request line is decoded.','Read TLS Record Layer Content Type: Application Data is 23.','The source is 198.51.100.81. Without decryption information, this capture does not reveal an HTTP path.'],'No. 2 comes from 198.51.100.81 and has TLS Application Data content type. It does not reveal HTTP Method, path, or body. The teaching payload is opaque synthetic data, not decryptable ciphertext from a real session, and it does not prove a successful TLS connection.',[['What is the source IPv4 address of the Application Data record?','The recorded IP source header remains readable despite opaque content.'],['Can this recording reveal the HTTP URL path without decryption information?','No. 2 is TLS Application Data with no decoded HTTP request line. Having a TLS record does not make its content readable.',{'all-readable':'No. 2 is an HTTP GET /index.html request, not Application Data','cannot-read':'Application Data is present, but the HTTP path cannot be read from this recording','root-path':'Application Data displays / as an HTTP Request URI'}]]);
L('app-tls-03','Some connections expose no name','Compare two ClientHellos: one includes SNI and the other does not. When an extension is absent, report observable information such as destination IP.','Select the packet with SNI and read the other connection\'s destination IP.',['Compare TLS extension lists in ClientHellos No. 4 and 9.','No. 4 has server_name=known.test. No. 9 has no server_name extension.','Select only No. 4 for SNI. No. 9 has IPv4 destination 198.51.100.83; no hostname guess is needed.'],'No. 4 has SNI known.test; No. 9 has none, but destination 198.51.100.83 remains visible. Missing SNI alone does not establish an anomaly, nor does an IP uniquely determine a hostname. Capture scope and encryption also affect visibility.',[['Select packets that actually contain an SNI Server Name field.','No. 4 has server_name; ClientHello No. 9 does not.'],['What is the destination IPv4 address of the ClientHello without SNI?','No. 9 still has an observable IPv4 header.'],['Which description of No. 9 is supported?','Inspect No. 9\'s extensions and destination. known.test and 198.51.100.82 belong to No. 4. ',{'limited':'This ClientHello has no SNI and targets 198.51.100.83','same-name':'This ClientHello has SNI known.test and targets 198.51.100.83','invalid':'This ClientHello has no SNI and targets 198.51.100.82'}]]);
L('grad-1','From joining the LAN to name resolution','A new device joined the LAN. You are asked to verify configuration and name resolution from the recording. Check the configuration acknowledgment and DNS response.','Report the assigned IP, DNS server, and resolved address with evidence.',['Read DHCP ACK, ARP, and DNS response in sequence.','Find Your IP Address and DNS option in ACK, then DNS Answers.','Use dhcp.ip.your and dhcp.option.domain_name_server in No. 4, and dns.a in No. 8.'],'DHCP ACK allocates 10.0.0.45 and DNS server 10.0.0.53. After ARP resolves the DNS server MAC, docs.test returns A record 192.0.2.80. This capture contains no web-access outcome.',[['Which client IP did ACK allocate?','Your IP Address in ACK gives the allocation.'],['Which DNS server IP was distributed?','Read the DHCP DNS Server option.'],['Which IP was returned as the A record for docs.test?','Read the IPv4 address in the DNS Answer.']]);
L('grad-2','Which stages succeeded?','A user reports that ping replies arrive but service.test:8080 cannot be reached. Examine each stage separately and avoid attributing a cause beyond the recording.','Check reachability, name resolution, and TCP connection results.',['Find ICMP Echo Reply, DNS Answer, and the TCP response to SYN.','No. 6 returns RST instead of SYN/ACK.','Use No. 2 for ICMP, No. 4 for DNS, and No. 6 for RST. This does not distinguish a stopped service from a firewall.'],'Echo Reply and a DNS A answer were observed, but SYN to TCP/8080 received RST/ACK, so this attempt did not establish a connection. The PCAP alone cannot establish whether no service was listening or a device on the path rejected it.',[['What can you verify about ping?','No. 2 is Echo Reply. This supports ICMP reachability, not normal application operation.',{'reply':'An Echo Reply was received from 192.0.2.80','all':'Destination Unreachable was received from 192.0.2.80','none':'An Echo Request is present but no corresponding reply is recorded'}],['What A record was returned for service.test?','The DNS answer returns 192.0.2.80.'],['What can you report about the TCP connection?','No. 6 is RST/ACK, distinct from SYN/ACK or FIN/ACK. Separate the reset observation from its internal cause.',{'ok':'SYN received SYN/ACK and the final ACK is recorded.','stopped':'SYN received FIN/ACK, starting normal closure.','reset':'SYN received RST/ACK; this connection attempt was not established.'}]]);
L('grad-3','Explain traffic with evidence','This recording contains plaintext HTTP and the start of a separate TLS connection from one client. Report HTTP results and limit TLS conclusions to observable facts. Follow TCP streams as needed.','Read the requested path, HTTP status, and what the separate TLS record establishes.',['Filter with http to find request and reply; clear it before inspecting TLS.','HTTP is in No. 6 and 7; TLS ClientHello is No. 12.','The HTTP path is /guide and status is 200. TLS SNI is secure.test, but no URL path or page completion is visible.'],'GET /guide goes to portal.test and receives 200 OK with body Welcome. A separate TLS ClientHello advertises secure.test. Later TLS traffic is absent, so this capture does not establish completed TLS, a URL path, or successful page display.',[['Which path was requested over HTTP?','Read HTTP Request URI.'],['What is the HTTP response code?','This request receives 200 OK.'],['What does the recording establish about the TLS connection?','No. 12 exposes SNI secure.test. portal.test belongs to the separate HTTP request. Later TLS traffic, URL path, and completion are not recorded.',{'complete':'ClientHello SNI is secure.test; a subsequent ServerHello is also recorded.','sni':'ClientHello SNI is secure.test; no subsequent TLS traffic is recorded.','same':'ClientHello SNI is portal.test; no subsequent TLS traffic is recorded.'}]]);
L('realworld-options','TCP options and raw sequence numbers','Real SYNs often include MSS, SACK Permitted, Window Scale, and Timestamps. The combination varies by OS and configuration. Even with a long Info column, find each field in the details.','Read MSS in No. 1 and compare relative and raw sequence numbers.',['Find MSS Value and both Sequence Number fields in TCP details of No. 1.','MSS proposes a maximum TCP data size, not a frame length including headers.','tcp.options.mss_val is 1460. tcp.seq is 0; tcp.seq_raw is a large 32-bit value.'],'MSS is 1460; Window Scale is 7 (multiplier 128), with SACK Permitted and Timestamps. Relative Seq 0 is a Wireshark display reference; tcp.seq_raw shows the actual initial value. Both SYNs negotiate Timestamps, so later ACKs also carry the option.',[['What MSS does No. 1 advertise, in bytes?','Read TCP Option MSS Value.'],['How do Sequence Number and Sequence Number (raw) compare in No. 1?','Distinguish the display baseline from the number stored in the header.',{'both-zero':'Both relative and raw values are 0; numbering begins with SYN/ACK.','relative':'The relative value is 0; the raw value is a different, large 32-bit number.','mss':'The relative value is 1460; the raw value equals the source port.'}]]);
L('realworld-extra','Additional information beyond Answers','DNS Additional records and DHCP Options supplement the main results. Find the needed settings without confusing them with DNS Answers or Your IP Address.','Read the UDP payload size in DNS OPT and the renewal time in DHCP ACK.',['DNS query is No. 5; DHCP ACK is No. 4.','Find DNS OPT and DHCP Option 58. Option 51 is the full lease time.','dns.rr.udp_payload_size is 1232; dhcp.option.renewal_time_value is 1800 seconds.'],'Additional RRs=1 in No. 5 refers to EDNS0 OPT, not one answered IP address. 1232 advertises the supported DNS UDP payload size. DHCP ACK distributes lease=3600, renewal=1800, and rebinding=3150 seconds. Discover also carries Client identifier, Host Name, Vendor class, and Maximum message size.',[['What UDP payload size does OPT advertise in No. 5?','OPT carries additional information including size; it is separate from Answer.'],['What is Renewal Time Value in No. 4, in seconds?','Read Option 58, distinct from Lease Time and Rebinding Time.']]);
L('realworld-time','Read timestamps and capture location','The time column supports relative seconds, absolute UTC, and the delta from the previous captured frame. Real Wireshark may show local Arrival Time; compare UTC Arrival Time or Epoch Arrival Time when correlating logs.','Switch time display, inspect No. 2, and explain 54 versus 60 bytes.',['Choose Previous-frame delta and select No. 2. Deltas use original recording order before filtering.','No. 2 has a Frame time delta and Ethernet Padding. Compare frame length in No. 1.','No. 2 has frame.time_delta=0.125 seconds and eth.padding=6 bytes. No. 1 has frame.len=54.'],'No. 2 is at UTC 2026-01-01 00:00:00.125, 0.125 seconds after the preceding frame. Without TCP options or data: Ethernet 14 + IPv4 20 + TCP 20 = 54 bytes. On the LAN, six padding bytes produce the 60-byte minimum excluding FCS. Sender-side processing can capture 54 bytes before padding. This comparison capture combines observation points.',[['How many seconds separate No. 2 from the preceding captured frame?','The delta uses the original capture order, not the preceding visible row after filtering.'],['Why is No. 1 54 bytes and No. 2 60 bytes in this example?','Compare decoded Frame Length and Ethernet Padding.',{'tcp-data':'Only No. 2 contains six TCP payload bytes; No. 1 has no application data.','fcs':'No. 2 includes four FCS bytes and two padding bytes; No. 1 omits both.','padding':'No. 2 includes six Ethernet padding bytes; No. 1 was captured before padding.'}]]);
L('realworld-dns','Find name resolution among 284 packets','A user reports errors opening reports.test from 10.0.0.42. Other configuration and device-discovery traffic shares the LAN. First isolate the target client\'s DNS exchange. The background traffic need not be deleted.','Show only DNS for 10.0.0.42 and read the response A record.',['dns alone may include discovery traffic. Add the client IP condition.','ip.addr matches both requests and responses.','dns && ip.addr == 10.0.0.42 leaves two packets. Use dns.a in the response.'],'No. 41 and 59 resolve reports.test to 198.51.100.42. Filters narrow the scope without reading every mDNS, SSDP, or ARP packet. Verify DNS success and HTTP success separately.',[['Show only the two DNS packets involving 10.0.0.42.','The target client\'s query and response remain.'],['Which A-record IP was returned for reports.test?','The DNS Answer identifies the next destination to investigate.']]);
L('realworld-stream','Isolate and follow one connection','After DNS, inspect TCP between 10.0.0.42 and 198.51.100.42. tcp.stream is a connection index Wireshark assigns within a capture. Read it from the target packet because indices vary across captures.','Show only the six target TCP packets and follow their HTTP exchange.',['Start with tcp && ip.addr == 10.0.0.42.','Read Stream index in the target TCP details. The other client\'s HTTP uses stream 0.','Apply tcp.stream == 1. Use Follow stream and the HTTP response code.'],'Stream index 1 contains six packets from SYN through request and response. The server returns HTTP 404 Not Found for /reports/latest. Do not confuse this with the 200 response on another client\'s stream 0.',[['Show only the six TCP packets in 10.0.0.42\'s HTTP connection.','The selected connection includes handshake through response acknowledgment.'],['What HTTP response code did the target connection receive?','Use the HTTP response in the target stream.']]);
L('realworld-contains','Find a request with a byte search','frame contains "GET" finds captured frames with uppercase GET bytes regardless of protocol. A match does not necessarily mean an HTTP request; check source and HTTP fields afterward. Split strings and encrypted content need other methods.','Show both GET matches and report the target client\'s requested path with evidence.',['Apply frame contains "GET". Matching is case-sensitive.','Compare the two sources and select the request from 10.0.0.42.','The target http.request.uri is /reports/latest, not the other client\'s /health.'],'No. 102 and 192 contain GET bytes. Client 10.0.0.42 requests /reports/latest; the other client requests /health. Byte search is a starting point. Correlating source, URI, and status within one connection supports the report: DNS resolved, then /reports/latest received 404.',[['Show only the two frames containing uppercase GET bytes.','Search actual captured bytes, not the protocol name.'],['Which HTTP path did 10.0.0.42 request?','Distinguish the target client\'s Request URI from the other connection.']]);
const chapters=Object.fromEntries([
['ops-view','Read the interface','Your first three steps from packet list to evidence.'],['ops-filter','Display filters','Narrow traffic using your own conditions.'],
['base-ethernet','Ethernet','Destinations for delivery on the same link'],['base-arp','ARP','Resolve an IPv4 address to a MAC address'],['base-ip','IPv4 / IPv6','Peers and packet lifetime'],['base-icmp','ICMP','Distinguish reachability checks from errors'],['base-icmp6','ICMPv6 / NDP','IPv6 Echo, neighbors, and router advertisements'],['base-udp','UDP','Datagram headers and application responses'],
['app-tcp','TCP','Connection setup, acknowledgments, and closure'],['app-dns','DNS','Names, answers, and transport protocols'],['app-dhcp','DHCPv4','Follow configuration offers and acknowledgments'],['app-ntp','NTP','Time exchanges and the limits of observation'],['app-http','HTTP','Read requests, responses, and reassembly'],['app-tls','TLS','Visible names and encrypted-content limits'],
['grad','Final investigations','Connect protocols and explain your findings.'],['realworld-basics','Skills for real captures','Explore options, time, and capture locations.'],['realworld-investigation','Investigate a busy LAN','Find the target client among 284 packets.']
].map(([id,title,subtitle])=>[id,{title,subtitle}]));
const captures=Object.fromEntries([
['ops-first','First name resolution and connection','Fictional traffic recorded at client 10.0.0.10, from DNS query to TCP setup. Relative time is measured from capture start.'],
['ops-mixed','A network shared by three clients','Fictional traffic from three clients at a mirrored port on the same LAN. Time is in relative seconds.'],
['base-eth-addresses','Ethernet source','Client 10.10.1.20 and server 10.10.1.30 on the same LAN exchange short UDP messages.'],
['base-eth-broadcast','Frames for the whole LAN','Unicast traffic is mixed with ARP queries for MAC addresses.'],
['base-eth-gateway','Web server and next hop','Client 10.10.3.20/24 uses gateway 10.10.3.1 to reach 192.0.2.80 on another network. Captured on the client LAN.'],
['base-arp-request','Whose MAC is being requested?','Client 10.20.1.12 queries 10.20.1.1 on the same LAN and receives a reply.'],
['base-arp-answer','Mappings in ARP replies','Client 10.20.2.25 requests printer 10.20.2.60\'s MAC. Another client\'s ARP is also present.'],
['base-arp-pair','Pair interleaved ARP messages','Client 10.20.3.15 queries file server 10.20.3.99 and gateway 10.20.3.1 in succession.'],
['base-ipv4-source','IPv4 source and destination','Local ping and TCP to another network, captured on the client LAN.'],
['base-ipv6-hop','IPv6 destination and Hop Limit','Client 2001:db8:30::25 sends UDP to 2001:db8:30::80 with Hop Limit set to 32.'],
['base-ip-transport','Separate IP from its contents','IPv4, IPv6, UDP, and ICMP are interleaved. Check IP version and the next protocol separately.'],
['base-icmp-echo','Ping requests and replies','10.40.1.10 sends Echo Requests to 10.40.1.1 with Sequence Numbers 7 and 8.'],
['base-icmp-unreachable','An unreachable UDP port','10.40.2.15 sends to UDP port 7099 at 10.40.2.50, which returns an ICMP message.'],
['base-icmp-ttl','A message from a router on the path','A UDP packet with TTL 1 targets another network. The first router stops forwarding and returns ICMP.'],
['base-icmp6-echo','Read ping over IPv6','2001:db8:50::20 sends an IPv6 Echo Request to 2001:db8:50::1.'],
['base-ndp-neighbor','Discover an IPv6 neighbor','fe80::20 discovers router fe80::1 on the same link. Another neighbor exchange is interleaved.'],
['base-ndp-router','An RA announces a router','Router fe80::1 sends an all-nodes Router Advertisement. Echo and neighbor discovery from other devices are included.'],
['base-udp-ports','UDP source and destination ports','Client 10.60.1.18 queries a simple UDP response service at 10.60.1.70:7070.'],
['base-udp-length','What does UDP Length include?','Short ASCII strings PING and PONG illustrate UDP Length versus Ethernet frame length.'],
['base-udp-retry','Request/response behavior over UDP','The example application repeats a request after one second. Only one response, after the second request, is recorded.'],
['app-tcp-01-capture','TCP connection setup','A short DNS/TCP capture containing the complete TCP three-way handshake.'],
['app-tcp-02-capture','TCP acknowledgments','Teaching traffic on TCP port 9000 exchanges five HELLO bytes and two OK bytes, with unrelated UDP interleaved.'],
['app-tcp-03-capture','FIN versus RST','Two TCP connections between the same hosts: one closes with FIN/ACK, the other receives RST after data.'],
['app-dns-01-capture','DNS name and IPv4 answer','An A-record query and answer with unrelated time synchronization for filter practice.'],
['app-dns-02-capture','AAAA and NXDOMAIN','DNS over IPv4 returns an AAAA answer and NXDOMAIN for a different name.'],
['app-dns-03-capture','DNS over TCP','Independent DNS exchanges over UDP and TCP, the latter captured from its handshake. This does not model fallback after a truncated UDP response.'],
['app-dhcp-01-capture','DHCP DORA','Four packets show initial DHCPv4 acquisition on one segment without a relay.'],
['app-dhcp-02-capture','DHCP configuration options','Eight interleaved packets from two DHCPv4 acquisitions with different transaction IDs, client MACs, and assigned IPs.'],
['app-dhcp-03-capture','Two DHCP offers','One Discover receives two offers. The client selects one server and receives ACK.'],
['app-ntp-01-capture','NTP request and reply','Name resolution followed by NTP. Teaching timestamps do not establish real current time or clock accuracy.'],
['app-ntp-02-capture','Advertised NTP strata','One client receives strata 2 and 3 from two servers. This is not a clock-accuracy comparison experiment.'],
['app-ntp-03-capture','A bounded NTP observation window','Two target NTP requests have no recorded replies. The finite capture also includes another client\'s NTP exchange and DNS.'],
['app-http-01-capture','HTTP GET and 200 OK','One HTTP/1.1 GET and a complete 200 OK response with matching Content-Length, including TCP setup.'],
['app-http-02-capture','HTTP POST and 404','Two HTTP/1.1 connections: 404 for GET and 201 for POST with a body. All messages are complete plaintext HTTP with Content-Length.'],
['app-http-03-capture','A split HTTP response','An HTTP response spans two TCP segments. Content-Length matches the entire body; HTTP is decoded after reassembly.'],
['app-tls-01-capture','TLS ClientHello name','A valid teaching ClientHello and its TCP acknowledgment. This does not demonstrate completed TLS or successful certificate verification.'],
['app-tls-02-capture','Opaque TLS content','A TLS Application Data record simulates a capture starting mid-session. Its opaque teaching bytes contain no real-session keys or decryptable ciphertext.'],
['app-tls-03-capture','ClientHello with and without SNI','Two valid teaching ClientHellos, one with SNI and one without. This does not implement ECH encryption.'],
['grad-connect','A new client joins the network','Fictional mirrored-LAN traffic from initial DHCPv4 configuration through DNS resolution.'],
['grad-failure','Reachable host, unavailable service','Ping, DNS, and TCP attempts recorded at client 10.0.0.21. No internal server logs are included.'],
['grad-web','Web results and visibility limits','HTTP and a separate TLS ClientHello captured at 10.0.0.30. No decryption information or subsequent TLS traffic is included.'],
['realworld-tcp-options','TCP options and relative numbers','Fictional TCP at 10.0.0.42. SYNs advertise MSS, SACK, Window Scale, and Timestamps. After both sides negotiate Timestamps, later ACKs carry them too.'],
['realworld-extra-records','Additional DNS and DHCP information','Teaching DHCP configuration and DNS from a LAN mirror. DNS OPT is additional information, not an answer address.'],
['realworld-time-padding','Timestamps and 54 versus 60 bytes','This comparison combines capture points for one connection. No. 1 and 3 represent sender-side capture before padding; No. 2 represents LAN capture with padding. All frames omit FCS. TCP options are omitted for this comparison.'],
['realworld-lan','Investigate a LAN with background traffic','About 28 seconds at a fictional LAN mirror: 270 background ARP, mDNS, SSDP, ICMPv6, other-client DHCP, and NTP packets, plus two DNS packets and twelve packets across two HTTP connections. The target is 10.0.0.42.']
].map(([id,title,description])=>[id,{title,description}]));
const glossary=[
['Packet / frame','A unit of network data. Each row here represents an Ethernet frame; expand its IP, TCP, and other inner layers.'],
['Capture','Traffic observed and recorded at a particular location and time. An absent packet does not prove the communication never occurred.'],
['Source / Destination','The sender and recipient of a packet. Directions reverse between requests and responses.'],
['MAC address','An address for delivery on the same link, serving a different purpose from an IP destination beyond routers.'],
['IP address','An address identifying an IP sender or destination. IPv4 and IPv6 use different notation and headers.'],
['Port number','TCP and UDP ports distinguish services and communication endpoints. A port alone does not prove an application. ICMP does not have TCP/UDP-style ports.'],
['Ethernet','A link-layer technology used on LANs. Inspect MAC addresses and EtherType.'],
['ARP','Resolves an IPv4 address to a MAC on the same link. Traffic to a remote server normally first uses the gateway MAC.'],
['TTL / Hop Limit','A value decremented at routers to prevent indefinite forwarding loops. It is not elapsed time in seconds.'],
['ICMP / ICMPv6','Control and error messages for IP, including Echo used by ping and Destination Unreachable.'],
['NDP / RA','IPv6 neighbor and router discovery using ICMPv6, distinct from IPv4 ARP. RA advertises routers and prefixes. IPv6 also supports SLAAC and DHCPv6 configuration.'],
['UDP','A datagram transport without TCP-style connection setup, ordering, or retransmission guarantees. Applications may implement their own requests, responses, and retries.'],
['TCP','A transport providing an ordered byte stream. SYN starts a connection; sequence numbers and acknowledgments track data.'],
['SYN / ACK / FIN / RST','TCP flags: SYN starts a connection, ACK acknowledges data, FIN ends one sending direction, and RST resets a connection. Interpret them in context.'],
['TCP Stream','A reassembled view of data from one TCP connection. One application message may span several packets. Following a stream does not decrypt encrypted data.'],
['DNS','Maps names to information. A records contain IPv4 and AAAA records IPv6. DNS can use UDP or TCP, and encrypted DNS also exists.'],
['DHCP','Distributes settings such as IP addresses and DNS servers. These exercises cover initial DHCPv4 acquisition; DHCPv6 has different message formats.'],
['NTP','A time-synchronization protocol. Inspect Mode and Stratum. A response alone does not prove the client clock is correct.'],
['HTTP','Exchanges resources through requests and responses. Plaintext examples here use HTTP/1.1 over TCP. Status codes do not determine whether activity is legitimate.'],
['TLS / SNI','TLS protects communications. SNI may announce a server name in ClientHello but is not always observable. URL paths and bodies may remain encrypted.'],
['QUIC / HTTP/3','HTTP/3 runs over QUIC using UDP, so web traffic does not always use TCP. This edition introduces these terms without packet exercises.'],
['Display filter','Shows matching packets from a loaded capture without deleting the original data. Capture filters used during collection have different purposes and syntax.'],
['Reassembled field','Information decoded by combining packets. It may have no corresponding byte range in the selected individual frame.']
].map(([term,description])=>({term,description}));
module.exports={chapters,captures,lessons,glossary};
